%S A400632 42,262,752,1772,3516,6480,10948,17808,27434,41246,59736,85052,117744,
%T A400632 160994,215332,285252,371234,479426,610100,771552,963652,1197308,
%U A400632 1471844,1801320,2184214,2638462,3161344,3775360,4476296,5292002,6216272,7283312,8484370,9860962,11401300,13155280,15107344
%N A400632 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-8, for n >= 8.
%C A400632 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400632 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400632 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 6 less than the maximum.
%C A400632 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400632 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400632 The fourth diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400632 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A400632/b400632.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 8..300</a>
%H A400632 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996 (preprint).
%H A400632 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00505-6">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, Nuclear Physics B 482 (1996), no. 3, 497-535.
%H A400632 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400632 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%H A400632 Wenlin Zhang and Haobo Ma, <a href="/A400429/a400429.pdf">Semi-meanders with a fixed winding deficit: the third and fourth diagonals of A400429</a>, 2026.
%F A400632 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-3), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400632 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400632 For this sequence, j = 4 and 2*j = 8.
%F A400632 D_j(2*j) = a(8) = A005315(j) = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400632 D_j(2*j+1) = a(9) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400633(10).
%F A400632 a(n) = P(n) + (-1)^n*E(n) + C_3(n) + C_8(n).
%F A400632 P(n) = (294*n^6 - 60*n^5 - 3895*n^4 - 232300*n^3 - 326739*n^2 + 16955410*n - 48570245) / 138240.
%F A400632 E(n) = (-5*n^4 + 44*n^3 - 289*n^2 + 358*n + 4473) / 3072.
%F A400632 C_3(n) = (4/27)*((n-2)*cos(2*Pi*n/3) + (n+4)*sin(2*Pi*n/3)/sqrt(3)).
%F A400632 m_4(t) = 2*cos(Pi*t/2).
%F A400632 m_8(t) = 2*m_4(t)*cos(Pi*t / 4).
%F A400632 C_8(n) = (m_4(n-1) - m_4(n) + 2*(m_8(n) - m_8(n-1) + m_8(n-2))) / 32.
%e A400632 Row n=12 of A400429 is 1828,5968,3516,810,74,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8,10. Since n-8=4, a(12)=3516, which is the fourth-to-last value.
%Y A400632 Fourth diagonal of A400429. Cf. A005315, A005316, A400630, A400631, A400633, A400634, A400635, A400636, A400637, A400638, A400639.
